        "The steel safety barrier on the open route (at the pavement edge and in the median) is taken with a height of {0} m: it blocks the line of sight only if the ray from the driver's eye to the object passes below its top at the barrier. The level of the barrier foot is taken from the 3D road model, so the crossfall (barrier on the lower pavement edge) and the vertical alignment are accounted for. On a constant longitudinal grade the height of the sight line above the pavement does not depend on the grade (eye and object drop equally), so the line passes over the barrier because the barrier foot is lower or because of a sag vertical curve. Bridge cornices and parapets, tunnel and cut & cover walls, and cut slopes from the 3D model block over their full height.\n",
